-10-25T17:42:00Z","updated_at":"2019-11-19T01:20:13Z","closed_at":"2019-11-19T01:20:13Z","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"# Summary\r\n\r\nWe watched a user run `streamlit hello` and see it fail in the dataframe example because `Pandas.isna()` wasn't defined.\r\n\r\n## Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n1. Install an old version of Pandas. (Sorry, we don't have a record of what it was.)\r\n2. Go to the dataframe demo in `streamlit hello`\r\n\r\n# Behavior\r\n\r\n## Actual behavior\r\n\r\nAn exception is thrown because `pd.isna()` wasn't defined.\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThe demo should work beautifully, of course ;) \r\n\r\n# Debug info\r\n\r\nThis was not recorded unfortunately, although it maybe worth noting that this user had `pip install`ed Streamlit into a Conda environment.\r\n\r\n# Possible Solution\r\n\r\nWe need to figure out the minimum version of Pandas which has `isna()`. Ways to solve this:\r\n1. Look up that function and figure out when it was introduced.\r\n2. **(Safer!)** install progressively earlier versions of Python and run the dataframe demo until we can find the incompatible version.","closed_by":{"login":"tvst","id":690814,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MDgxNA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/690814?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst","html_url":"https://github.com/tvst","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tvst/orgs","repos_url":"https: